{"potential_benefits": " Potential benefits include stronger response to the treatment protocol, decreased risk of metastasis, and improved survival time. ", "potential_risks": " Poor response to treatment  Higher than described adverse effects from combination treatment (nausea, diarrhea, weight loss, bone marrow suppression, illness requiring hospitalization and management) ", "diagnosis": "Hemangiosarcoma", "inclusion_criteria": "     Biopsy confirmed hemangiosarcoma       Life expectancy greater than 2 weeks       No major organ failure          ", "exclusion_criteria": "     Evidence of renal or liver failure that precludes use of doxorubicin or carboplatin       Patients taking antioxidant supplements (or these may be discontinued during the study)     Patients who have previously received both doxorubicin and carboplatin as part of their treatment (if they have received only one of the two options, then they can be considered for the other arm)     ", "intervention_name": " Doxorubicin   Carboplatin   Temozolomide   Propranolol ", "pri_outcome_name": "Control of disease and survival", "pri_outcome_measure": "Imaging will be used to monitor disease status and owner questionnaires (along with labwork and physical examinations) will be used to monitor dogs. Modified RECIST criteria will be used to characterize tumor response.", "pri_outcome_endpoint": " The study will be halted for patients that experience progressive disease. Median time to progression and median survival times will be described. Owners may also elect to remove their dog from the study at any point. ", "sec_outcome1_name": "Adverse effects", "sec_outcome1_measure": "VCOG-CAE 2020 will be used to measure and describe adverse events. Bloodwork, imaging, patient monitoring/examinations, and owner questionnaires will all be utilized to determine adverse events.", "sec_outcome1_endpoint": " Dogs that experience high grade (grade 3-4) adverse events will have interventions to try to eliminate those adverse events (fluid therapy, hospitalization, medical management). Reduction of chemotherapy doses will be considered as well in order to improve tolerance of treatments. Dogs that experience a decline in heart function during doxorubicin treatment will no longer receive doxorubicin treatment but may be considered for the carboplatin arm. ", "sec_outcome2_name": "", "sec_outcome2_measure": "", "sec_outcome2_endpoint": "", "study_results": "", "funding_source_names": "", "funding_source_government": null, "funding_source_foundation": null, "funding_source_company": null, "funding_source_institution": null, "funding_source_none": true}
